Your own settings and keys. Everything here except this file and
network.example is git-ignored: it is baked into the images, so keep the
images private too.

  network                       settings shared by every device
                                (copy network.example and fill it in)
  private-ca-certificates.crt   your private CA certificates (one or more,
                                PEM), trusted by every device and its Alpine
  ssh_keys                      public SSH keys that can log in as root, one
                                per line (the contents of ~/.ssh/<key>.pub)
  devices/<any name>            one file per device
  tailscale/<name>              a device's tailscaled.state, copied after
                                its first login (tailscale_state below)
  raptor                        the update server login for scripts/publish.sh:
                                  username = admin
                                  password = ...

A device file: "name = value" lines, # starts a comment line.

  model     = 3040            3040 or 5020
  host      = shop-01         host name
  address   = 192.168.80.13   IP for scripts/deploy.sh and test-device.sh
  tailscale_state =           empty for a new device: it boots logged out.
                              Log in by hand on the device:
                                tailscale up --ssh --netfilter-mode=off --hostname=<host>
                              open the link, turn off key expiry on the
                              Machines page, then copy
                              /var/lib/tailscale/tailscaled.state to
                              tailscale/<name> and write <name> here

The build adds the device's keys at the end (ssh_key, netbird,
update_token: its login to the update server;
see scripts/device-keys.sh). Keep a copy of the files: without these keys a
device gets new VPN identities.

Your private SSH key stays out of this folder. Keep it on your computer
(for example ~/.ssh/wyse_ed25519, made with
  ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C wyse -f ~/.ssh/wyse_ed25519)
and load it into the SSH agent on the Mac:
  ssh-add --apple-use-keychain ~/.ssh/wyse_ed25519
VS Code passes the agent to the container; deploy and test use it.
